  "body": "**Need (from a user, in our words):** Users keep one insert and many holders that accept it. Today each CamCraft tool is a separate whole, so the same insert is defined again in every holder. Wanted: a component library (inserts, holders, adapters, extensions), each with its own mounting coordinate system; holders state which inserts they accept; an assembly is built from components and stays linked, so a change to a component updates every assembly that uses it. Parametric tools remain and can serve as components.\n\n**Reference:** ISO 13399 (cutting tool data representation: tool items, adaptive items, coordinate systems and connection interfaces). Clean-room: no competitor material.\n\n**Related:** #1039 (lathe holder bodies placed by ISO 13399 coordinate systems), #1040 (tool bodies assembled from several vendor models), #2092 (a tool's library holder becomes a reference - the first step toward linked components).\n\n**Open questions:**\n1. Data model: which component kinds (insert, holder, adapter, extension, collet/chuck); which ISO 13399 coordinate systems and connection codes do we need?\n2. Compatibility: how does a holder declare the inserts it takes (ISO insert shape, size, seat), and how do we refuse a wrong combination by name?\n3. Linking: library references vs copies in a project; what happens when a linked component changes; project-file format and migration (relates to #2092).\n4. Geometry: how the assembly's cutting edge, gauge line and body (for collision checks) are derived from the components' frames (relates to #1039, #1040).\n5. Milling too? Indexable face mills and end mills use the same idea (body plus inserts).\n6. UI and MCP: how users browse \"holders that take this insert\", and the commands needed (Everything Is a Command).\n\n**Done means:** each question answered in a comment, a recorded design decision, and concrete sub-issues filed (each with P/effort/area), then this umbrella is closed.\n",
